<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shimabara Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1637">1637</a>–<a href="/years/1638">1638</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shimabara Rebellion was an uprising of peasants, many of them Christians, and masterless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> on the Shimabara Peninsula and the Amakusa islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> in 1637–1638, provoked by heavy taxation, famine and persecution. Led nominally by the youth Amakusa Shirō, the rebels held Hara Castle until it was stormed by a shogunal army of over 100,000 men. Its suppression was followed by the expulsion of the Portuguese and a stricter ban on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shimabara-rebellion.jpg" alt="Shimabara Rebellion" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shimabara-Battle-Map-c17th-century.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
